# Kratos LLM Configuration Example
# Copy this to .env and adjust values as needed

# Model context window for the server. Default is 6144 -- verified (2026-07-11)
# to comfortably cover a full agent/loop.py ReAct investigation (measured
# ~4095 tokens used by iteration 8/10 at the old 4096 default, which left
# ~0 headroom and correlated with the small model losing track of its own
# response format late in a run). Only applies to the direct llama-cpp-python
# load path (KRATOS_LLM_BACKEND=llama_cpp) -- the OpenAI-compatible path
# below (the default, e.g. Ollama) manages its own context window
# server-side; this setting has no effect there. Raise further if you
# increase MAX_TOKENS_QUESTION or see truncation on longer investigations;
# lower to save RAM if you don't need the full ReAct agent loop.
KRATOS_LLM_N_CTX=6144

# Request timeout in seconds (for inference queries)
KRATOS_LLM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900

# Maximum tokens to generate in analysis responses
KRATOS_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=2048

# Maximum tokens for question responses. Increase this when you want a
# longer answer for `kratos chat -q ...`.
KRATOS_LLM_MAX_TOKENS_QUESTION=512

# Enable fallback to direct model loading when server unavailable
# Set to 1 (default) to allow fallback, 0 to disable
KRATOS_LLM_FALLBACK_TO_DIRECT_LOAD=0

# Optional: Custom model path (if not using default) -- only relevant if you
# explicitly select the direct llama-cpp-python load path below.
# KRATOS_LLM_MODEL_PATH=/path/to/model.gguf

# LLM backend (2026-07-15 refactor: unified base-url/api-key/model wiring).
# KRATOS_LLM_BACKEND: "llama_cpp" (direct in-process load of the local GGUF
# file above) or "openai_compatible" (HTTP, via the 3 vars below -- local
# Ollama, another local server, or a cloud provider). Leave unset ("auto")
# to prefer llama_cpp if a local GGUF file exists, else openai_compatible.
# KRATOS_LLM_BACKEND=openai_compatible

# LLM_BASE_URL / LLM_API_KEY / LLM_MODEL: the ONE real wiring mechanism for
# any OpenAI-chat-completions-compatible endpoint. Defaults (used when these
# are left unset/commented, as here) point at a local Ollama server with
# zero configuration needed -- this is the shipped backend. IMPORTANT: these
# are no longer a separate opt-in-only "fallback" path -- setting them here
# makes them the ACTIVE default on every invocation, not just "available
# for later". For occasional cloud-backend testing, export the real values
# per-invocation instead of leaving them in .env:
#   export LLM_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai
#   export LLM_API_KEY=your-api-key-here
#   export LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.5-pro
#   export KRATOS_LLM_BACKEND=openai_compatible
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=ollama
# LLM_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b

# Optional: Run LLM server in background for faster repeated queries
# Start with: kratos llm-serve
# Then use: kratos chat (will use running server)

# Optional: threat-intel enrichment (see CLAUDE.md's "Decision: threat-intel
# enrichment scope" for the full moat-tension reasoning). Two tiers, and
# setting a key below does NOT by itself activate anything -- opt-in means
# opt-in, not "configured".
#
# OTX_API_KEY: used ONLY by the scheduled/manual cache-refresh step
# (update_threat_intel_cache()), never during a live investigation. Powers
# the default tier -- AlienVault OTX pulses synced to a local cache on your
# own schedule; lookups during an investigation read that cache only, no
# live call, regardless of whether this key is set.
# OTX_API_KEY=your-otx-api-key-here
#
# ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY: used ONLY by the opt-in LIVE escalation tier, and only
# when BOTH KRATOS_THREAT_INTEL_ENABLED=1 below AND a human approves that
# SPECIFIC lookup at a real-time prompt (same approval gate as
# run_linux_command, asked every time, never remembered/skipped). Setting
# this key alone does not cause any live network call.
# ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY=your-abuseipdb-api-key-here
#
# KRATOS_THREAT_INTEL_ENABLED: master opt-in switch for the LIVE escalation
# tier specifically -- the cached OTX tier needs no flag, since it never
# makes a live call during an investigation either way. Default 0
# (disabled): Kratos ships with zero live threat-intel calls possible out
# of the box. Set to 1 to make the live-escalation approval prompt
# reachable at all -- it still asks every time; this flag alone never
# grants unattended access.
# KRATOS_THREAT_INTEL_ENABLED=0
